By default, requests are attributed to the owner of the gateway key. To attribute them to the person using the agent, send their email in the `X-User-Email` header.

```ts
const client = new OpenAI({
  baseURL: "https://gw.armoriq.ai/v1/t/<gateway-key>/a/<agent-id>",
  defaultHeaders: { "X-User-Email": "dana@example.com" },
});
```

The gateway reads the header and does not forward it to the provider.

`Stop` first atomically renames the live `.ndjson` file to a `.ndjson.shipping` file. This gives the `Stop` process exclusive ownership of the claimed file before it reads anything. It then:

1. reads the metadata and all complete span records;
2. starts a fresh in-memory `ObservabilityRecorder`;
3. replays the spans under a new recorder-owned trace ID;
4. adds transcript token deltas as generation spans;
5. ends the trace and awaits the POST to `/observability/spans`;
6. removes the claimed file.

Because the hook process is short-lived, it cannot rely on the SDK's five-second periodic timer. The explicit flush is required for delivery. It also means an unavailable backend can make `Stop` wait for retries even though failures never change the policy outcome.

If the shipper exhausts its retries, the batch is dropped and the claimed file is removed. If the `Stop` process fails before cleanup, the `.shipping` file remains but is not treated as a durable retry queue.

Route: `/dashboard/observability/traces`

Use the trace list to find slow, denied, or failing operations across sessions. The filters are:

* status: **OK**, **Error**, or **Denied**;
* a **Has error** toggle;
* search by trace name;
* service name (exact match) and instrumentation scope (exact match);
* minimum duration in milliseconds.

The table has these columns: Time, Name, Task outcome, Technical status, Latency, Tokens, Cost, Spans, Tags, and Session. The Spans cell can show **degraded trace** or an error count.

The list requests the first 100 traces from the last 7 days. It has no Load more control. When nothing matches, it says "No traces match" and suggests that you widen the time frame or clear the filters.

* A **session** is one continuous agent run or conversation. It has one stable UUID.
* A **trace** is the set of spans that belong to one run. The SDKs make one trace for each session.
* A **span** is one timed step inside a trace.

A trace contains spans such as:

* `armoriq.agent.run`, the root span of the run;
* `armoriq.intent.plan`, the plan that the agent made;
* `armoriq.policy.evaluate`, one for each policy decision;
* `gen_ai.chat`, one for each model call;
* `armoriq.tool`, `mcp.execute`, `bash.execute`, `cli.execute`, and `command.execute`, for tool and command work;
* `armoriq.approval.wait` and `armoriq.delegation.wait`, when a hold waits for a person.

See the [data model](/platform/observability/data-model) for the attributes.

**Change history** lists recent changes to the settings, newest first. Columns are When, Who, Change, and Detail. It records:

* changes to the data collection setting, and the start and end of debug capture;
* legal hold turned on or off, and external content export allowed or blocked;
* scope overrides saved or removed;
* alert rules created, changed, or turned off;
* trace export requested, completed, downloaded, expired, or failed;
* data deletion completed;
* SDK policy lease issued (hidden by default in the API).

Only people who can view the settings see this section.

If you call the session API yourself, you already open and close the session. That same close ends the trace. You still add no span code and no flush code.

